# Build context exclusions for the frontend image.
#
# This file has to live HERE, not at the repo root. Docker reads .dockerignore from the root of the
# BUILD CONTEXT, and compose gives this image `context: ./frontend` (it is the odd one out — the
# root-module Go services build from `context: .`). So the root .dockerignore, which already lists
# node_modules and .next, is never consulted for this image.
#
# Without these exclusions the Dockerfile silently builds the wrong thing. Its builder stage does:
#
#     COPY --from=deps /app/node_modules ./node_modules   <- the tree npm ci installed from the lockfile
#     COPY . .                                            <- clobbers it with whatever is on the host
#
# so a developer's local node_modules overwrites the one built from package-lock.json. That is not
# hypothetical: it broke every boot script on this machine after Next moved 16.2.x -> 16.3.x. The
# host had 16.2.11 installed, the lockfile pinned 16.3.1, and `next build` died with
# `Error: Missing field turbopackMemoryEviction` — the 16.2 binary reading a 16.3 config. Because
# all four compose boot jobs build this image, the E2E and all four failure proofs failed together,
# and the error named a Turbopack config field rather than the version skew that caused it.
#
# CI never saw it: a fresh checkout has no node_modules and no .next. That is exactly what makes it
# worth excluding explicitly — the bug only exists on the machines where someone is actually working.
node_modules
.next

# Never bake secrets or local state into an image layer.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Noise: nothing below is read by `next build`, and every byte of it is uploaded to the daemon.
.git
.gitignore
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
# go.mod here is a fence, not a build input — it keeps the root Go module's `./...` out of
# node_modules. See the comment inside it; the divergence it fixes is the same shape as the
# node_modules one above, and hid in the same place.
go.mod
*.md
npm-debug.log*
.DS_Store
coverage
